1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,110
Your store is not ready for agents. They are already shopping.

2
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:18,070
Agent traffic to Shopify storefronts is up eight times year over year, and seventy eight point six percent of agent carts are abandoned. That is a customer who never reaches you, and no dashboard shows you which door was locked.

3
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:34,555
So we asked one hundred and forty eight real storefronts for their catalogue, the way a shopping agent asks. Seventy sent nothing back at all. Sixty seven sent a feed with no barcode in it. Every tile on this wall is one real shop, and empty means empty.

4
00:00:35,105 --> 00:00:47,715
Eleven of them do have barcodes. Glossier, Tatcha, Brooklinen, Alo Yoga, Away. They publish to Shopify's Catalog protocol, and their own storefront hides it. Your shopper's agent reads the storefront.

5
00:00:48,265 --> 00:00:58,775
This is what that agent reads on your product page. Not one bar is scannable. There is nothing to scan, and nothing to match to a product an agent can actually buy.

6
00:00:59,325 --> 00:01:14,285
Six sandbox storefronts, each broken a different way. A CAPTCHA on checkout. A forced account login. A price that disagrees with the feed. Out of stock only at the till. Paste your own domain and your tile joins the wall in under a minute.

7
00:01:14,835 --> 00:01:28,020
This store scores seventy out of one hundred. Every point on the bill is priced from a published abandonment share, and every line names its source. Fix one thing, re-audit the same URL, and we print the delta.

8
00:01:28,570 --> 00:01:45,130
The proof is WebMCP. Eighteen tools registered on document dot model context, in this open tab. Your assistant adds to the same order you are looking at, human and agent in one cart, and prepare checkout validates the total and never charges a card.

9
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:49,140
ReadyCounter. Agent commerce, now reviewable.

